],[8,8,8,8,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,4,4,4,4,4,4,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"## How the puzzle works\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Every grid holds several small \"figures\" on a plain background. Each figure has a **frame** drawn in colour 1, which is a plus in pair 1 and an X in pair 2. Attached to the frame are **decorations** in other colours:\n- In pair 1, the decorations are the centre cell, a stem and a base.\n- In pair 2, they are the centre cell plus one extra cell either above or below the X.\n\nSome figures are drawn at a larger scale: every cell is blown up into a 2×2 or 3×3 block.\n\n**What changes.** Ignoring scale, the figures in each pair carry exactly two kinds of decoration. One figure is the odd one out and the rest share the other kind.\n- **Pair 1:** the odd figure has stem 8 and base 8 8 8. The other two have stem 2 and base 3 3 3. In the output they trade: the odd one gets stem 2 and base 3, and both others become all 8, including the 2×-scale figure.\n- **Pair 2:** the odd figure has a 6 above its X. The others have a 4 below. In the output the odd one loses its 6 and gains a 4 below. The others lose their 4 and gain a 6 above, scaled 2×2 on the big figure.\n\nSo the rule is: **the colour-1 frame stays put, and the non-1 decoration pattern is swapped between the two groups.** Each pattern is measured relative to the frame's bounding box in frame units, so it is redrawn at each figure's own scale. The centre cell counts as decoration too. In the training pairs all centres are 2, so swapping them changes nothing there. In the test the odd figure's centre is 6 while the others have 3, so the centre colour travels with the rest of the decoration.\n\n**Scale.** A figure's scale is its frame bounding-box size divided by the smallest frame's size, computed separately for each axis.\n- Pair 1: the frames are 2×3 and 4×6, so the scale is 2.\n- Test: the frames are 3×4 and 9×12, so the scale is 3.\n\n**Test input.**\n- The odd figure is the one at x 9–12: centre 6 6, with 4 4 below.\n- The other three figures (top-left, big, right) have centre 3 3, with 2 2 above.\n- After the swap, the odd figure gets centre 3 3 and 2 2 above.\n- The other three get a 6 centre and 4 4 below. On the big figure this is a 6×3 centre block of 6 and a 3-row block of 4 beneath the frame.\n\n**Construction stages emitted.**\n1. Erase every decoration, leaving only the frames.\n2. Stamp the swapped decoration onto each figure, one figure at a time.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    FR = 1\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg, diagonal=True, multicolour=True)\n    info = []\n    for o in objs:\n        fr = [(y, x) for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"] if g[y, x] == FR]\n        if not fr:\n            continue\n        info.append({\"cells\": o[\"cells\"], \"fbox\": bbox(fr)})\n    if not info:\n        return g\n    minh = min(i[\"fbox\"][2] - i[\"fbox\"][0] + 1 for i in info)\n    minw = min(i[\"fbox\"][3] - i[\"fbox\"][1] + 1 for i in info)\n    for i in info:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = i[\"fbox\"]\n        sy = max(1, (y1 - y0 + 1) // minh)\n        sx = max(1, (x1 - x0 + 1) // minw)\n        i[\"s\"] = (sy, sx)\n        t = {}\n        for (y, x) in i[\"cells\"]:\n            if g[y, x] != FR:\n                t[((y - y0) // sy, (x - x0) // sx)] = int(g[y, x])\n        i[\"tmpl\"] = frozenset(t.items())\n    from collections import Counter\n    cnt = Counter(i[\"tmpl\"] for i in info)\n    if len(cnt) != 2:\n        return g\n    (a, _), (b, _) = cnt.most_common(2)\n    other = {a: b, b: a}\n    out = g.copy()\n    for i in info:\n        for (y, x) in i[\"cells\"]:\n            if g[y, x] != FR:\n                out[y, x] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Erase all decorations; keep only the colour-1 frames.\")\n    H, W = out.shape\n    for i in info:\n        y0, x0, _, _ = i[\"fbox\"]\n        sy, sx = i[\"s\"]\n        for (dy, dx), c in other[i[\"tmpl\"]]:\n            for a2 in range(sy):\n                for b2 in range(sx):\n                    y = y0 + dy * sy + a2\n                    x = x0 + dx * sx + b2\n                    if 0 <= y < H and 0 <= x < W:\n                        out[y, x] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Stamp the swapped decoration (scale {sy}x{sx}) onto the figure at frame {i['fbox']}.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Scale.** A figure's scale is its frame bounding-box size divided by the smallest frame's size, computed separately for each axis."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Erase all decorations; keep only the colour-1 frames."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Stamp the swapped decoration onto each figure, one figure at a time."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamp the swapped decoration (scale 1x1) onto the figure at frame (1, 12, 2, 14)."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Stamp the swapped decoration (scale 1x1) onto the figure at frame (5, 3, 6, 5)."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The centre cell counts as decoration too."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"ation pattern is swapped between the two groups.** Each pattern is measured relative to the frame's bounding box in frame units, so it is redrawn at each figure's own scale."}],"scale":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Some figures are drawn at a larger scale: every cell is blown up into a 2×2 or 3×3 block."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What changes.** Ignoring scale, the figures in each pair carry exactly two kinds of decoration."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"tput they trade: the odd one gets stem 2 and base 3, and both others become all 8, including the 2×-scale figure."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"35766d4ffc23010b2dca2a93344b545f4275fbdd60dbc36fc4751412554622a8","task_sha256":"88de33df68c1dab37a3d7f9a2fcefd330c9d9950d62ede9559590ab1c30708da","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
